Title: "Sets, Elements, and the Subset Relation"
Id: "6a5f2490-ada4-47d3-98ea-c640ff3a5e43"


Text: "Set theory begins with the intuitive notion of a set as an aggregate of
       definite, distinct objects of thought or perception into a unified whole.
       Formulated by Georg Cantor in 1895, this foundational discipline provides
       the ontology upon which all modern mathematical structures are erected.
       The fundamental relationship between an object x and a set A is
       elementhood, denoted x \in A."
Id: "3a1b02c6-9b42-49dd-bca2-ba1b572d37e5"


[\set.builder{x}:such.that{P}]
Defines: S is \set
when:
. x is \\anything
. P is \\statement
Documented:
. called: "set-builder comprehension $\{x \mid P(x)\}$"
. written: "\{x \mid P(x)\}"
. description: "The collection of all objects x satisfying the property P(x)."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{2}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{4}
Id: "80890007-c804-4fa3-9290-dfe48b5944ca"


Text: "A set is entirely characterized by the elements it contains, without
       regard to order, repetition, or internal structure. This fundamental
       principle is the Extensionality Principle: two sets are equal if and only
       if they possess exactly the same elements."
Id: "68a75c06-9082-443f-b9cc-ab188a8fa4d1"


[\extensionality.equality{A, B}]
States:
when: A, B is \set
that:
. forAll: x
  then: ((x "in"? A) \.iff./ (x "in"? B))
Documented:
. called: "extensional equivalence of sets"
. written: "\forall x\,(x \in A \leftrightarrow x \in B)"
. description: "The condition stating that sets A and B share identical members,
                which by extensionality implies A = B."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{2}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{2}
Id: "ab793ae4-4007-4dec-ab7e-5421540f4447"


Text: "The simplest set is the empty set, denoted \emptyset, possessing no
       elements whatever. By the Extensionality Principle, any two sets having
       no elements must be equal; hence the empty set is unique."
Id: "6df807fe-87f5-4624-80e2-38c13a559869"


Theorem:
given: A, B is \set
where:
. forAll: x
  then: (x \.not.in./ A)
. forAll: x
  then: (x \.not.in./ B)
then: (A = B)
Documented:
. called: "uniqueness of the empty set"
. written: "A = \emptyset \land B = \emptyset \implies A = B"
. description: "By the Principle of Extensionality, any two sets possessing no
                elements are identical."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{4}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{8}
Id: "a7e0c01d-b7b0-4a53-a718-5797d5efa1ee"


Text: "Beyond membership, the primary comparison between two sets is
       containment. Set A is a subset of set B, written A \subseteq B, if every
       element of A is also an element of B. It is vital to distinguish between
       primitive elementhood (x \in A) and subset inclusion (A \subseteq B)."
Id: "eb8cde50-39ee-4b0b-9396-e11c138ab8ea"


[A \.subseteq./ B]
States:
when: A, B is \set
that:
. forAll: x "in" A
  then: (x "in"? B)
Documented:
. called: "subset relation $A \subseteq B$"
. written: "A? \subseteq B?"
. description: "Set A is a subset of set B, meaning every element of A is also
                an element of B."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{5}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{11}
Id: "77076483-512a-4e52-ba80-67514dc0ea46"


Theorem:
given: A, B, C is \set
where:
. A \.subseteq./ B
. B \.subseteq./ C
then: (A \.subseteq./ C)
Documented:
. called: "transitivity of subset inclusion"
. written: "A \subseteq B \land B \subseteq C \implies A \subseteq C"
. description: "The subset relation is transitive across all sets."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{5}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{12}
Id: "9a221ca4-9915-4c71-babc-686f00d528c3"


Text: "When containment holds without equality, the smaller set is strictly
       contained within the larger. We say A is a proper subset of B, written A
       \subsetneq B, guaranteeing the existence of an element in B that does not
       belong to A."
Id: "be69c934-cebd-455e-9940-db93d7778e6d"


[A \.subset.proper./ B]
States:
when: A, B is \set
that:
. have: (A \.subseteq./ B)
. have: (A != B)
Documented:
. called: "proper subset $A \subsetneq B$"
. written: "A? \subsetneq B?"
. description: "Set A is strictly contained in set B: A \subseteq B and A \neq
                B."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{6}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{12}
Id: "7131e709-9181-4991-abfc-d06f6551033d"


Text: "Given any set A, we can form the collection of all its subsets. This
       collection is the power set of A, denoted \mathcal{P}(A). If A has n
       elements, its power set \mathcal{P}(A) possesses 2^n elements."
Id: "396ccdec-6c14-4d09-aa52-a708a8d771f6"


[\power.set.of{A}]
Defines: P is \set
when: A is \set
Documented:
. called: "power set $\mathcal{P}(A)$"
. written: "\mathcal{P}(A?)"
. description: "The set whose elements are all the subsets of set A."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{6}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{19}
Id: "9765f0b8-d94d-40e6-a5a0-ea374b863475"


Theorem:
given:
. A is \set
. E is \set
. P is \set
where:
. E = \empty.set
. P = \power.set.of{A}
then:
. E "in"? P
Documented:
. called: "empty set belongs to the power set"
. written: "\emptyset \in \mathcal{P}(A)"
. description: "Because the empty set is a subset of every set, \emptyset is an
                element of \mathcal{P}(A) for every set A."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{6}
Id: "e33b662f-0b08-4b5b-a62f-a0db82c2bc15"


Text: "In early formulations of set theory, unrestricted comprehension was taken
       for granted: any condition P(x) was assumed to determine a valid set \{x
       \mid P(x)\}. In 1901, Bertrand Russell revealed a fatal flaw in this
       assumption by examining the set of all sets that do not contain
       themselves: R = \{x \mid x \notin x\}.

       Evaluating whether R belongs to itself produces the immediate
       contradiction R \in R \iff R \notin R. This result, Russell's Paradox,
       demonstrated that unrestricted comprehension is inconsistent,
       necessitating the rigorous axiomatic foundations of Zermelo--Fraenkel set
       theory."
Id: "a2469238-6eb0-419e-9e52-286e7132889f"


[\russell.set]
Declares: R is \set
Documented:
. called: "Russell's set $R = \{x \mid x \notin x\}$"
. written: "R = \{x \mid x \notin x\}"
. description: "The collection of all sets that do not contain themselves as
                members, formulated by Bertrand Russell to demonstrate the
                inconsistency of unrestricted comprehension."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{1}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{6}
Id: "58f6f7cb-cc45-4838-a25f-943fa1b57f0c"


[\russell.paradox]
Theorem:
given: R is \set
then: ((R "in"? R) \.iff./ (R \.not.in./ R))
Documented:
. called: "Russell's Paradox"
. written: "R \in R \iff R \notin R"
. description: "The foundational contradiction of naive set theory establishing
                the impossibility of unrestricted set comprehension."
References:
. $enderton.set.theory:page{1}
. $halmos.set.theory:page{7}
Id: "9fbe7f26-365c-491e-86e2-92328740db2a"
